    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#margaret-i-of-denmark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Margaret I of Denmark" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I of Denmark</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_of_Denmark,_Norway_%26_Sweden_(1389)_effigy_2010_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacob Truedson Demitz assisted by Emil Eikner for Ristesson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> grew out of the Kalmar Union, the union of the three Scandinavian crowns formed in 1397 under Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a>. In 1523 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a> broke away under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>, and in the same year the Danish nobility deposed the Union king Christian II and elected his uncle, Duke Frederick of Schleswig-Holstein, as Frederick I; Norway accepted him in 1524. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a> and Norway thus remained under one king of the house of Oldenburg, together with Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and the Norse claims to Greenland, while the king also ruled the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein. After Frederick’s death in 1533 a civil war, the Count’s Feud of 1534–1536, pitted supporters of the imprisoned Christian II, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lubeck">Lübeck</a>, against Frederick’s son Christian III. Christian III’s victory brought the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>: in 1536 the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> were arrested and their lands confiscated by the crown, and a Lutheran church ordinance followed in 1537. Christian III’s accession charter of 1536 declared that Norway should no longer be a separate kingdom but a part of the Danish realm, a change made effective when the last Catholic archbishop of Nidaros, Olav Engelbrektsson, fled the country in 1537. Norway nonetheless kept its own laws and much of its own administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#north-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-sea.jpg" alt="North Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#north-sea">North Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA_NorthSea1_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MODIS Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The realm combined two very different lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scania">Scania</a> east of the Sound until 1658, was a fertile agricultural country of grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, dominated by noble estates worked by tenant farmers; from 1733 the <em>stavnsbånd</em> tied Danish peasant men to the estates where they were born, until the agrarian reforms of 1788 abolished it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> was a land of freehold and tenant farmers, fishermen and foresters, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a>, sawn in water-powered mills, was exported in large quantities to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>, and whose dried and salted fish from the north was traded through Bergen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">Silver</a> mines at Kongsberg, opened in 1623, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> mines at Røros, from 1644, added to royal revenue. In the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#census">census</a>, of 1769, Denmark had roughly 800,000 inhabitants and Norway about 720,000. Copenhagen, the capital, grew into the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The crown drew a large income from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sound-toll">Sound Toll</a>, levied at Helsingør on ships passing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic. The Lutheran church, staffed by university-trained pastors, shaped education and daily life, and Pietism gained royal favour in the eighteenth century. Danish trading companies dealt in Asian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tea">tea</a>, textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-indies">West Indies</a>, much of it re-exported.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-fredriksten"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten.jpg" alt="Siege of Fredriksten" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-fredriksten">Siege of Fredriksten</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bringing_Home_the_Body_of_King_Karl_XII_of_Sweden,_1884_CE,_by_Gustaf_Cederstr%C3%B6ms,_Nationalmuseum,_Stockholm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> fought a long series of wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a> for control of the Baltic and its approaches. The Northern Seven Years’ War of 1563–1570 ended in stalemate at the Treaty of Stettin, and in the Kalmar War of 1611–1613 Christian IV took Älvsborg and forced Sweden to pay a heavy ransom for it at the Treaty of Knäred. Christian IV’s intervention in the Thirty Years’ War ended in defeat by Tilly at Lutter am Barenberge in 1626. In the Torstenson War of 1643–1645 Swedish armies overran Jutland, and by the Treaty of Brömsebro Denmark-Norway ceded Gotland, Ösel and the Norwegian provinces of Jämtland and Härjedalen. Worse followed in 1658, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of Sweden marched his army across the frozen Belts and imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>, Trøndelag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bornholm">Bornholm</a>; Copenhagen survived a Swedish assault in 1659, and Trøndelag and Bornholm were returned in 1660. The navy was the realm’s main strength: Niels Juel defeated the Swedes at the Battle of Køge Bay in 1677, and Peter Tordenskjold won at Dynekilen in 1716 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. A separate Norwegian army was organised from 1628, and fortresses such as Kronborg, Akershus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a> guarded the realm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Until 1660 the king governed with the Council of the Realm, the <em>Rigsråd</em>, drawn from the high nobility, and each new king signed an accession charter limiting his powers. The disasters of 1658–1660 changed this. At a meeting of the estates in Copenhagen in 1660, clergy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burghers">burghers</a>, resentful of noble privilege, joined Frederick III in making the monarchy hereditary, and in 1661 he became absolute ruler. The King’s Law, the <em>Kongeloven</em> of 1665, defined this absolutism in writing, the only European constitution of its kind. Government was reorganised into colleges, or departmental boards, staffed increasingly by commoners and German-speaking officials, and a new nobility of counts and barons was created. Christian V issued uniform law codes, the Danish Code of 1683 and the Norwegian Code of 1687. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> was governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> or <em>stattholder</em> in Christiania and kept its own law and army. Absolutism allowed rapid reform but also abuse: in 1770–1772 the royal physician Johann Friedrich Struensee ruled in the name of the mentally ill Christian VII, issuing hundreds of decrees, including freedom of the press, before he was overthrown and executed. Later reforming ministers abolished the <em>stavnsbånd</em> in 1788 and decreed in 1792 an end to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, effective from 1803.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hans-egede"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-egede.jpg" alt="Hans Egede" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hans-egede">Hans Egede</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Poulsen_Egede.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johan Hörner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> left a lasting imprint on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>. Its Lutheran church and absolute monarchy shaped the political culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a> and Norway, and the Danish written language became the language of administration and literature in Norway, the basis of the later Bokmål standard. Ludvig Holberg, born in Bergen, wrote comedies and histories that founded modern Danish-Norwegian literature, and the astronomer Tycho Brahe, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> at his observatory of Uraniborg on Hven, made the most precise naked-eye observations of his age; Ole Rømer first measured the speed of light in 1676. The realm also built a modest overseas empire: Tranquebar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> from 1620, forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, and St Thomas, St John and St Croix in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-indies">West Indies</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations relied on enslaved Africans. The Danish decree of 1792 made Denmark-Norway the first European state to ban the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> by law. The missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hans-egede">Hans Egede</a> began the Danish recolonisation of Greenland in 1721, the origin of the present link between Denmark and Greenland. Christian IV’s building projects, including Rosenborg, the Round Tower and the new Norwegian capital of Christiania, founded in 1624, still mark the cities of both countries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> prospered as a neutral trading nation during the wars of the late eighteenth century, but its neutrality brought conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a> joined the League of Armed Neutrality, a British fleet under Hyde Parker and Horatio Nelson defeated the Danish fleet at the Battle of Copenhagen in 1801. In 1807, fearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a> would seize the Danish navy, Britain bombarded Copenhagen and carried off the fleet, driving Denmark-Norway into alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>. The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naval-blockade">blockade</a> cut Norway off from Danish grain and caused famine in 1808–1809, and the state went bankrupt in 1813. After Napoleon’s defeat, Frederick VI was forced by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kiel">Kiel</a> in January 1814 to cede Norway to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a>, while Denmark kept Iceland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and Greenland, which had been Norwegian dependencies. Norwegians refused to accept the transfer: an assembly at Eidsvoll adopted a constitution on 17 May 1814 and elected the Danish prince Christian Frederick as king of an independent Kingdom of Norway. After a brief war with Sweden, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#convention-of-moss">Convention of Moss</a> led to a personal union, Sweden-Norway, in November 1814. The Norwegian constitution survived, and the separation from Denmark became the foundation of modern Norway, independent in 1905.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> joined two Scandinavian kingdoms under the Oldenburg kings for nearly three centuries, building a Lutheran absolute monarchy, a strong navy and a small overseas empire while losing the struggle for the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a>, and its break-up in 1814 shaped modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a>, Norway, Iceland and Greenland.</em></p>

